            <div xml:id="d88">

                <pb facs="0223" n="127" xml:id="pg127"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">195/874</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Herrick</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-11-05"><hi rendition="#italic">November 5,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received November 16.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 5682</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my telegram No. 540 of
                    November 3rd, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="51" xml:id="d88-f1"/> I have the honor to
                    enclose herewith a copy and translation of a note from the Foreign Office dated
                    November 3rd,<anchor n="51" xml:id="d88-f2"/> in which it is stated that
                    American ships touching at French ports will experience no difficulties until
                    such a time as an agreement has been made between France and the United States
                    concerning the equivalence of navigation certificates.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Myron T. Herrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
